e matching among re","title":"Deep CORAL: Correlation Alignment for Deep Domain Adaptation","version":"arxiv-v1"},{"document_id":"doc-1706.04599","snippet":"This classification calibration study distinguishes prediction confidence from empirical correctness, reports miscalibration in modern networks, and evaluates post-processing methods such as temperature scaling. Calibration fitting uses a labeled validation set, unlike label-free target adaptation. It is useful background against equating confidence with correctness, but is not a direct experiment on adaptation of deterministic regression models. Neither calibration quality nor increased confide","title":"On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks","version":"arxiv-v1"},{"document_id":"doc-1505.07818","snippet":"Domain-adversarial learning trains representations to support labeled-source prediction while reducing a domain classifier's ability to distinguish source and target examples. A gradient-reversal component implements the adversarial update. The study uses source examples and unlabeled target examples during training, so it is not directly a source-free test-time method. Domain indistinguishability and task-discriminative structure play different roles. This broader adaptation prior art is releva","title":"Domain-Adversarial Training of Neural Networks","version":"arxiv-v1"},{"document_id":"doc-2002.08546","snippet":"SHOT studies source-free unsupervised domain adaptation for classification: retain the trained classifier while adapting a target feature extractor without source examples. Information maximization combines confident individual predictions with diversity across the target collection; self-supervised pseudo-label refinement is another component. Its setting is not identical to one-pass adaptation of a scalar regressor. Class probabilities, class diversity and pseudo-label definitions must be dist","title":"Do We Really Need to Access the Source Data? Source Hypothesis Transfer for Unsupervised Domain Adaptation","version":"arxiv-v1"},{"document_id":"doc-2006.10726","snippet":"The v1 study adapts a pretrained predictive model using unlabeled target inputs, without source examples. Its objective reduces predictive entropy and its adjustable components are feature-wise affine modulations. Experiments concern image/digit classification. The method discussion explicitly also allows compatible probabilistic regressors with predictive variance; therefore 'entropy adaptation is categorically impossible for regression' misstates this source. That observation does not automati","title":"Fully Test-time Adaptation by Entropy Minimization","version":"arxiv-v1"},{"document_id":"doc-1909.13231","snippet":"Test-time training jointly prepares a main prediction task and an auxiliary self-supervised task sharing a feature extractor. At deployment the auxiliary loss updates shared parameters using unlabeled input. Rotation prediction is the image-specific example, not a universal requirement. Standard per-instance and stateful online variants differ in whether updates are retained. The paper gives a convex-model discussion involving gradient alignment; this is not an unconditional guarantee of improve","title":"Test-Time Training for Out-of-Distribution Generalization","version":"arxiv-v1"}],"result_hash":"dade0559bff101a8fc6f35f0d6c3047fc559522558826e008217d848b92e3acd"},"previous_hash":"e3092f36be2d4a448eaa75119707f9de1a0ea2cccd880408e0dcd6fdeecf9c92","sequence":17,"time_utc":"2026-09-23T05:48:48.168903+00:00"}
